WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.110
Film Courage: Hollywood films are built on
three components?

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.100
Character, desire and conflict?

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.459
Michael Hauge: Yes, they are!

00:00:06.459 --> 00:00:08.590
Film Courage: Okay, so Hollywood films [are
built on this].

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:09.610
Any other types of films?

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:12.240
[How about] Art house films…we talked about
briefly off camera?

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.480
Do the same rules apply, but maybe it’s
just different?

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.380
Michael Hauge: Well in that case there is
nothing different at all and we’re not just

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.869
talking about movies.

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:22.929
That’s the foundation of story.

00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:24.029
Any story.

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:26.019
You can talk about an epic poem?

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:29.300
You can talk about a fairy tale or children’s
book.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.570
It’s all built on character desire and conflict.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:38.120
Every story that I can think of, if it’s
a narrative story, that has a beginning, middle

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.420
and an end, is going to be about a character
who desperately wants something and something

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:44.560
stands in their way.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:48.620
Film Courage: And you say the desire moves
the story along but what we actually resonate

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:49.880
with is conflict?

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:56.560
Michael Hauge: I’m saying the conflict is
more responsible for the emotion that is illicit

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:02.290
because…well let me put it this way and
give an example of a movie.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:07.950
Well let’s say I said to you let’s go
to a movie this weekend.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.549
And you say “Well what are we going to see?”

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:15.030
I heard about this movie and it’s about
a guy who want to make a speech.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:20.009
Well chances are (even though I know you’re
into art house movies), you are probably going

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:22.859
to say “Yeah, but what else is playing?

00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:27.950
Watching a guy give a speech, you can do that
on TV and be bored to tears anytime.”

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:35.759
It’s a story about a guy who wants to give
a speech and he happens to be a prince in

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:43.200
Great Britain who has a horrid speech impediment,
who was abused as a child, and has no desire

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.479
for the throne but then has to become King
when his brother abdicates and the speech

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:53.329
he wants to give is going to be the speech
that leads his country into World War II.

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:58.439
Now it’s not only sounding intriguing, it’s
sounding like it could be an Oscar-winner

00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:04.009
because all of those things I just said were
all of the conflict he faces in trying to

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:05.009
give a speech.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:11.090
It’s not giving that speech that makes the
story exciting, emotional, appealing or commercial.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.650
It’s the obstacles he has to overcome to
do that.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.319
Film Courage: Let’s take desire, so in something
like FATAL ATTRACTION, which I know is an

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:21.819
extreme version of desire.

00:02:21.819 --> 00:02:24.250
The desire is not really what is moving the
story along?

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:30.230
It’s conflict in terms of…Michael Hauge:
No…no…desire is moving it forward but

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:33.950
you have to pinpoint what is…first of all,
who is the hero in the movie?

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.379
The hero is the Michael Douglas character,
okay?

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:44.799
And at the set-up he’s restless and not
a very moral character and then as the opportunity

00:02:44.799 --> 00:02:50.810
he meets Glen Close (I don’t remember the
character’s name) and he has an affair with

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.019
her and starts doing that.

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:57.120
So somewhere around the one quarter mark I
think he’s already gone to bed with her,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.780
but then we see that she might be a little
more possessive than he was anticipating.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:10.189
And gradually the more her craziness and possessiveness
and psychosis starts coming to the fore, the

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:17.909
more he wants to get away from her and finally
stop her from terrorizing him and his family.

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:25.860
So actually his outer motivation is to stop
the crazy woman he slept with from terrorizing

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.040
or destroying his family.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:34.319
And if you realize that the goal is to stop
her, then his desire to do that pushes that

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:35.500
story forward.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:42.069
Now admittedly it’s her actions that he
must react to or are moving things forward

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:43.069
just as much.

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:48.549
So he’s reactive more than proactive in
a way but what we’re rooting for is for

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:54.909
him to get away from this crazy woman before
everything goes KABOOM.

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:57.211
Even though we know he made a big mistake
we still are rooting for him.

00:03:57.211 --> 00:03:57.229
Question for the Viewers: What’s the best
movie you’ve seen this year?

